%S A398282 32,-1,-1,-1,203,-1,111,89,175,527,41,-1,63,91,616,271,95,133,1037,
%T A398282 217,203,45,39,131,-1,349,131,83,53,79,327,617,155,49,297,153,89,-1,
%U A398282 40,47,259,-1,417,-1,255,65,93,411,315,39,167,199,69,145,17,62,113,37,-1,77,63,39
%N A398282 Let Ab_k be the set of abelian groups of order k, M_k the set of rings Z/mZ such that phi(m) = k, and let f_k : M_k -> Ab_k be the map such that f_k(Z/mZ) = (Z/mZ)*, the group of units of Z/mZ. a(n) is the least r such that f_k is injective for k = p*2^r, p the n-th prime. a(n) = -1 if no such number exists.
%C A398282 The map f_k can be considered as a refinement of Euler's phi function. For example, we may define another map g_k: Z -> Z where g_k(m) = phi(m) = k, i.e. the map mapping all m to their totient value. The image of g_k is obviously the singleton {k}; g_k is trivially surjective, and Carmichael's conjecture is that it is never injective. Since the image of f_k contains those unit groups (Z/mZ)* such that |(Z/mZ)*| = k, f_k exhibits richer mapping properties.
%C A398282 Let p be the j-th prime. A sufficient condition that a(j) = -1 is that there are at least two Fermat primes F_i <= 65537 such that p*(F_i - 1) + 1 is prime (these primes are listed in A398299). See the Englezou link for a proof.
%C A398282 A057192(i) + 1 is a lower bound on a(i) for i such that prime(i) is neither a Fermat prime nor a Sierpinski number. If prime(t) is a Sierpinski number, A057192(t) = -1 = a(t). See the Englezou link for a proof.
%H A398282 Miles Englezou, <a href="/A398282/b398282.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..294</a>
%H A398282 Max Alekseyev, <a href="https://oeis.org/wiki/User:Max_Alekseyev/gpscripts">PARI/GP Scripts for Miscellaneous Math Problems</a> (invphi.gp).
%H A398282 Miles Englezou, <a href="/A398282/a398282_2.pdf">Proof of a sufficient condition for noninjectivity of f_k</a>
%e A398282 a(1) = 32 since f_{2^32} is injective, and 32 is the first such exponent where this is the case.
%e A398282 a(5) = 203 since f_{11 * 2^203} is injective, and 203 is the first such exponent where this is the case.
%e A398282 To illustrate a case where a(n) = -1, let n = 4, and thus p = 7. We have the following primes: 7*(17 - 1) + 1 = 113, and 7*(5 - 1) + 1 = 29. For r >= 4 + 2 = 6, define m_1 = 29 * 17 * 2^(r - 4 - 2 + 1) = 493 * 2^(r - 5), and m_2 = 113 * 5 * 2^(r - 4 - 2 + 1) = 565 * 2^(r - 5). It follows that phi(m_1) = 448 * 2^(r - 6) = 7 * 2^6 * 2^(r - 6) = 7 * 2^r = phi(m_2). Since (Z/m_1Z)* = C_28 x C16 x C_{2^r-2} = C_4 x C_7 x C_16 x C_{2^r-2} and (Z/m_2Z)* = C_112 x C_4 x C_{2^r-2} = C_4 x C_7 x C_16 x C_{2^r-2}, therefore f_k is noninjective for k = 7 * 2^r, r >= 6, and, by the linked proof, for r >= 1.
%o A398282 (PARI)
%o A398282 isinjective(n) = my(V = invphi(n), m = Map(), G); if(#V == 0, return(0)); for(i = 1, #V, G = znstar(V[i])[2]; if(mapisdefined(m, G), return(0)); mapput(~m, G, 1)); 1;
%o A398282 a(n) = my(F = [3, 5, 17, 257, 65537], p = prime(n)); if(n == 3 || vecsum(apply(x -> isprime(p * (x-1)+1), F))>=2, return(-1)); for(r = 1, oo, if(isinjective(p*2^r), return(r))) \\ using _Max Alekseyev_'s invphi.gp
%Y A398282 Cf. A019434, A057192, A394532 (numbers m such that f_m is surjective), A398299.
%K A398282 sign,new
